  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection.",
